==================================================
Lab 6: SQL Injection UNION Attack - Retrieving Multiple
Values in a Single Column
=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT

This lab is similar to the previous one, but with one extra challenge. The query here returns two columns, but
only one of them actually accepts text. In the last lab we had two text-friendly columns, so we could place
username in one and password in the other. Here, only one column works for text, so both username and password
need to be squeezed into that single column at the same time.

Goal: use a UNION attack to retrieve all usernames and passwords, even though only one column can hold text, and
then use the administrator’s credentials to actually log in.

==============================
WHY A NEW TRICK IS NEEDED HERE

In the previous lab, two columns could both hold text, so username went into one column and password went into
the other, like a neat two column table. In this lab, only one column is capable of holding text. If we try to
place username in one column and password in another like before, the database throws an error on whichever
column cannot hold text, and the whole UNION attack fails.

So instead of spreading username and password across two separate columns, we need to combine them together
into one single piece of text, then place that combined text into the one column that actually works.

=============================
THE TECHNIQUE: STRING CONCATENATION

Joining two pieces of text together into one is called concatenation. Different databases use different symbols
for this. In this lab, two vertical bar symbols written together, like this: ||, are used to join text.

Using this, username and password can be joined into a single combined value. A separator character is placed in
between them, so once we get the result back we can still tell where the username ends and the password begins.
In this script, a tilde symbol, which looks like this: ~, is used as that separator.

So instead of two separate columns of data, the result becomes one column containing something like:
administrator followed by a tilde, followed by the administrator’s actual password.

======================================
WHAT THE PAYLOAD LOOKS LIKE

The category value sent is: a single quote, then UNION SELECT, then NULL, comma, then username, two vertical
bars, a tilde in single quotes, two more vertical bars, then password, then FROM users, then two dashes.

The first column stays NULL since it cannot hold text. The second column holds the combined username and
password text, joined together with a tilde in between, pulled directly from the users table.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY

Open the lab and go to a product category page.
Submit a single quote, then UNION SELECT NULL, username || ‘~’ || password FROM users--
The page will now show a line containing every username and password combined together, separated by a tilde.
Find the line for the administrator account, split it at the tilde to get the real password.
Go to the login page and log in using administrator and that extracted password.
Once logged in successfully, the lab is marked as solved.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES

It imports the requests library, for sending web requests, and the re library, for searching and replacing text
using patterns.

A session is created so cookies stay consistent across every request made during the script, including the
login step at the end.

It asks for the category filter address, and from that same address it also builds the login page address, by
replacing the word filter with login, since both pages share the same base website address.

It builds the payload using a single quote, UNION SELECT, NULL for the first column, then username joined with
password using two vertical bars and a tilde in between, then FROM users, then two dashes to comment out the
rest of the original query.

This payload is sent as the category parameter to the filter page through the session.

It prints the status code, just to confirm the request went through.

It cleans the response HTML the same way as the previous lab’s script, removing script and style blocks first,
then converting all remaining tags into newlines, then removing empty lines.

It then searches through all the cleaned lines, and keeps only the ones containing a tilde symbol, since that
symbol only appears in our injected combined value, not in normal product names or prices.

If no lines contain a tilde, it prints a failure message and stops the script completely.

If some lines are found, it prints all of them, so you can see every username and password combination that
was leaked, not just the administrator’s.

It then specifically searches those lines for the one that starts with the word administrator followed by a
tilde, since that is the account we actually want to log in as.

If that specific line is not found, it tells you to copy one manually from the printed list, and stops.

If found, it splits that line into two parts using the tilde as the dividing point, giving us the username and
password separately, and prints them clearly.

It then reuses the same CSRF token grabbing method from lab two, fetching the login page and searching its HTML
for the hidden csrf field.

It builds a login payload using the csrf token, the extracted username, and the extracted password, and submits
it to the login page using a POST request through the same session.

It then builds the home page address by removing the word login from the login page address, and requests that
home page using the same session, so it is viewed as the logged in administrator.

Finally, it checks the home page content for the sentence the lab displays when solved. If found, it prints
success. If not, it prints a failure message suggesting the extracted credentials might be wrong.

=============================
WHY WE SPLIT ON THE TILDE SYMBOL

Since username and password were joined together using a tilde in between them during the database query
itself, that same tilde is still present in the text we get back on the page. By searching for that tilde and
splitting the text at that exact point, we can separate the combined value back into its two original parts,
username and password, without needing to guess where one ends and the other begins.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS

The underlying flaw is the same as every earlier lab, user input is joined directly into the SQL query text
without being checked. This time, string concatenation inside the UNION SELECT is used to work around the
limitation of only having one text compatible column, allowing two separate pieces of data to be squeezed
through a single column anyway.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS

Use parameterized queries so that user input can never change the structure of the SQL query, regardless of how
creatively it tries to combine multiple values together.

Other good practices:

Restrict database account permissions so the account used by the application cannot read sensitive tables such
as users, if it only ever needs to read product related tables.
Avoid displaying raw query results directly on public facing pages, since this makes it much easier to notice
when unexpected or combined values appear.

=============================
KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER

When only one column accepts text, multiple values can still be extracted by combining them together into a
single column using string concatenation.
A separator character placed between the combined values makes it possible to split the result back into its
original parts afterward.
Extracting credentials through a UNION attack is often just the first step, since the real goal is frequently to
use those credentials to log in and gain further access, as done in this lab.
